  "worked_solution": "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \u27e82\\pi \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\pi t),\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi t)\u27e9\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\u27e8\\int 2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\pi \\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi t)+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\pi t)+{C}_{2}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(3)=\u27e80,0\u27e9.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3) & =\u27e8-2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3\\pi )+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3\\pi )+{C}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e80,0\u27e9 & =\u27e82+{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}\u27e9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-2$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=\u27e8-2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi t)-2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\pi t)\u27e9.\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=\\frac{1}{2},$ we substitute $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\u27e8-2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})-2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e80-2,1\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-2,1\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
